<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #the-frame>>

! THE FRAME — no global now, and two readers who never share one

Two readers stand over the same bytes. TiddlyWiki's parser walks the whole source, looks ahead to
its end, and answers about a document. A TextMate grammar reads one line, carries a rule stack
across the newline, and answers about a line.

''Neither reader holds a global now.'' The grammar's now spans one line and whatever its stack
remembers; the parser's now spans the file. Each knows only //as of my own reach//, and no
engineering closes the distance, because the distance names what the two machines ARE.

So the founding sentence reads: ''agreement names a SYNC, never a truth.'' Where the readings
coincide, two islands synced. Where they part, a difference opens a ''candidate'' — McKeeman's
word, and he meant it strictly: //the results of two tested programs may differ and yet still be
correct//.

A harness counting disagreements as defects reclassifies every light-cone difference as a
regression. A harness ruling them by hand reclassifies them as whatever the hand felt that
afternoon. This design takes a third road: it ''measures which kind a divergence carries''.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #the-decidable-test>>

! THE DECIDABLE TEST — reach, measured, and one-sided by construction

Take any divergence standing at offset `i`.

''The grammar's reach at `i`'' spans the line holding `i`, plus the rule stack the lines before it
carried in. Reach runs BOTH ways, and each way ends somewhere different. Forward reach stops at the
line's end, because a pattern sees one line and no more. Backward reach travels only as far as a
rule stack carries, and a stack carries regions rather than the parser's own configuration.

!! Two arms, and both alterations derive

''The forward arm'' hands back the text a cut removed — the file's own remainder, replaced after the
sentinel. The sentinel keeps its offset; only what lies past it changes.

''The backward arm'' strikes the pragma lines, spelled by the token each pragma rule opens on and
harvested off that rule's own `matchRegExp`. A `\rules` run narrows the parser's active rule set and
no stack carries that.

Neither alteration reads a list anybody typed, and both earn their place: measured over this
corpus, the forward arm moves 14 verdicts and the backward arm moves 8. A forward-only probe misses
every one of those 8, and the pragma case stands as the worked counterexample — its verdict holds
under every alteration after the line and moves the moment the pragma changes.

!! What a moved arm proves, and what a still arm does not

* An arm ''MOVES'' the parser's verdict -> the deciding evidence sits outside the grammar's reach ->
the divergence ''REACHES OUT''. Permanent by construction, and filing one as owed names a repair
nobody can perform.
* No arm moves -> ''UNPROVEN'', and that names the absence of a proof and nothing else. An
alteration carries a closer only where the file happened to hold one, so a still arm reports no
evidence rather than evidence of reach. Reading its silence as //the evidence sat within reach//
would state a verdict on nothing — the move this whole probe exists to retire.

''So the gate runs one-sided and says so:'' nothing filed as owed may reach out. A ruling of
structural standing over an unproven class reports as a JUDGEMENT, marked as one, refusing nothing
and awaiting a sharper arm.

The probe sorted the ledger on its first run: one entry filed as owed reached past the grammar, and
two rulings stood without proof and stand marked.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #two-axes>>

! TWO AXES, NEVER ONE COUNT

A pseudo-oracle asks whether two readings match. Two readers computing different functions never
satisfy that outright, so the mismatch gets absorbed into a growing ledger and the growth carries a
structural source rather than a defect source. Program-analyser differential testing settled this
by splitting the verdict, and the split maps onto the two defect families this grammar already
names.

* ''SWALLOW'' — the grammar assigns LESS structure than the parser. An under-approximation. A
region runs past a boundary the parser closes, and constructs after it lose their reading.
* ''INVENTION'' — the grammar names a scope where the parser names none. An over-approximation. A
verdict fires on markup standing in plain sight, or a construct colours where the parser refused.

''Two ratchets, opposite polarity, each with its own collision.'' One count fusing them lets either
hide the other: a swallow retired and an invention added reads as no change at all.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #the-specimen-law>>

! THE SPECIMEN LAW

!! Cut inside the line

A TextMate engine tokenizes line by line and carries its stack across the newline, so a cut at a
line boundary lands where that machine behaves most benignly — never mid-pattern, never splitting a
multi-line opener, never leaving a partial match straddling the boundary. ''A generator cutting
only at line boundaries avoids by construction the positions where the two readers most plausibly
part.''

Measured over this corpus: cutting at line boundaries finds 27 divergences across 9 classes;
cutting at character offsets inside a line finds 2111 across 38, and ''29 of those classes the line
cut never reaches at all'' — headings, lists, inline macro calls, six widget families, start tags,
XML declarations, shortcuts.

A mid-line cut also models the only reader who matters: a grammar earns its living on text under a
cursor, and text under a cursor sits unterminated almost continuously.

!! Vary the sentinel

One sentinel held fixed across every specimen sits as an uncontrolled constant. The reading at an
offset must not depend on WHICH construct follows it, so the sentinel varies and the invariance
gets asserted rather than assumed.

!! Omit features, never only sample them

A corpus cut from real text carries every construct in real proportion, and real-world frequency
diverges from defect-finding frequency. Swarm testing measures the gap: diversity through feature
OMISSION found 42% more distinct compiler crashes than a tuned default, because some features
actively suppress the behaviour a run hunts, and features compete for room inside one specimen.

!! Ratchet diversity, never count

A specimen costs nothing here, so a floor over specimen count rises forever while finding nothing.
The ratchet rides the ''tuple of observed behaviours'' — the grammar's scope, the parser's node and
the position — and a specimen earns its place by producing a tuple nobody has seen.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #workstreams>>

! THE SIX WORKSTREAMS

Each names what it asserts, the collision proving it can refuse, the ratchet it carries, and the
thing it MUST NOT do.

!! W1 · The character-offset sweep

''Asserts:'' at every character offset inside every corpus line, the two readers agree about the
span at a known position, or the ledger explains the difference.
''Collision:'' a grammar stripped of one bound spelling draws reports the ledger does not cover.
''Ratchet:'' two disagreement counts, swallow and invention, each falling only.
''Must not:'' fuse the two counts, and must not cut only at line boundaries.

!! W2 · The engine differential

A grammar green under one regex engine runs wrong under another, silently, because the host
swallows what the engine refuses — probed here, a scanner accepts `(?<unclosed` and `a{2,1}` alike
and raises nothing, so the swallow sits below the API and a second engine has to answer.

''Asserts:'' every pattern this repository ships reads identically under each engine a reader may
run — the WASM Oniguruma the editor loads, and a JavaScript translation of the same syntax.
''Collision:'' a pattern using a construct only one engine implements draws a report.
''Ratchet:'' a count of patterns whose readings part, falling only.
''Must not:'' treat either engine as the authority. Both readings stand; the DIFFERENCE reports.

!! W3 · The must-fail corpus

Two senses live in one exclusion list today, and a list fusing them cannot stay readable: a
specimen written to be malformed, and a specimen the grammar cannot yet handle.

''Asserts:'' a specimen filed as must-fail DOES fail, and a specimen filed as a known divergence
carries a reason, an owner and an expiry.
''Collision:'' a must-fail specimen the grammar starts reading correctly fails the gate — a
positive control that passes reports a control that stopped controlling.
''Ratchet:'' the known-divergence list falls; the must-fail list grows freely.
''Must not:'' hold both senses in one file.

!! W4 · Generated collisions

A hand-written collision proves a gate refuses for ONE reason somebody already imagined. The fault
this whole harness exists to catch reappears here, one level up, inside its own tests.

''Asserts:'' each gate kills a machine-generated mutation of the grammar — an `end` dropped, a
character class widened, a `begin` anchor flipped, two patterns reordered, a bound removed.
''Collision:'' the mutation score itself, measured against a gate deliberately weakened.
''Ratchet:'' mutation score, rising only, and read as a FLOOR rather than a target, since the score
correlates imperfectly with real fault detection.
''Must not:'' retire the hand-written collisions. A generated mutant probes what the generator
imagines; the two populations differ.

!! W5 · Ledger expiry and ownership

The largest running deployment of this design carries three files rather than one — the
expectations, the ones that sat too long, and the ones nobody will fix. A ledger run long enough
produces a stale-divergence file whether or not anyone plans one.

''Asserts:'' every ledger entry carries a reason, an owner, the probe that classified it and an
expiry; a structural entry expires never, a defect entry expires and re-adjudicates.
''Collision:'' an entry past its expiry fails the gate.
''Ratchet:'' the count of defect-class entries falls; the structural class carries no ratchet,
because a light-cone difference retires only when a reader changes shape.
''Must not:'' let the disagreement count fall by ledger growth. That reads identical to falling by
repair and means the opposite.

!! W6 · The fuzzer

The oracle stands in-process, so generation costs almost nothing: generate, feed both readers, diff.

''Asserts:'' no generated input produces a divergence outside a ruled class.
''Collision:'' a seeded fault the fuzzer must find within a bounded budget.
''Ratchet:'' distinct behaviour-tuples reached, rising only.
''Must not:'' seed only from the corpus. The empty seed has out-yielded valid seeds in measurement,
and a seed set drawn from one corpus inherits that corpus's blind spots whole.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #what-this-declines>>

! WHAT THIS DESIGN DECLINES, AND WHY

* ''The render stage as an oracle.'' A rendered output answers what text MEANS once every reference
resolves; a grammar answers what text LOOKS LIKE on local evidence alone. The two coincide across
an easy middle and part exactly where a grammar errs, so the oracle stays quiet where it matters
and speaks where nobody can act. Probed: an undefined macro call PARSES and renders NOTHING, and a
grammar must colour it.
* ''The round-trip property.'' A TextMate grammar reconstructs no source. Locality, monotonicity and
invariance transfer; round-trip names a property this artifact cannot carry.
* ''A naming gate where structure already wins.'' The grammar reads filter operators and run
prefixes structurally, so an unknown name colours correctly and a list would demand maintenance for
nothing. Behaviour gets asserted; names do not.
* ''One disagreement count.'' See #two-axes.
* ''An auto-blessed snapshot.'' A blesser recording whatever the run produced converts a check into
a memory of the last run. Where an update path exists it refuses to bless a specimen whose
reference-parser side reports an error.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #standing-ground>>

! THE GROUND THIS STANDS ON

Searched from outside and returned empty: no project runs a TextMate grammar and a reference parser
as a live differential oracle; no prior art names a truncation sweep of a highlighter; no rule-level
coverage tooling exists for TextMate grammars; no academic literature covers testing syntax
highlighters against reference parsers.

''Read that twice.'' It grants novelty and it withholds a map. Nobody has published these failure
modes, so every one arrives here first — which raises the price of each instrument reporting green,
and raises the price of every collision left unwritten.

One measured caution carries more weight than the silence. The author maintaining a reference
markdown parser, its conformance fixtures AND the finest markdown TextMate grammar in existence
tests that grammar against twelve hand-curated snapshots. The differential sat within his reach and
he declined it. ''Cost signal, never permission.''

<<~/ahu>>

